Oh, I must have mixed up Vanilla behavior with Slash'Em or some
variant. 

> I've wrested quite some wands in the past; wishing was not on the top
> of my list. Off the top of my head a wand of cold can be very helpful.
> Inspecting now the objects.c file I can add (in no particular order):
> create monster, striking, digging, polymorph. Generally speaking; if
> you have no charging and need/want to achieve something like smashing
> a blocking boulder, creating food or sacrifice fodder, escaping (while
> locked in a room that's besieged by monsters) polypile or polymorph
> your pet; just for some examples. It's not a typical action (1 in 121
> is far too bad for that), but the wrest supported me occasionally.

Yes, but if you could you would rather charge those wands.

>> The wrest-feature has been with NetHack with the same probability
>> (why 1/11^2 ?) since it was Hack.
>
> (Don't know why 1 in 121.) But it need not be that value. As I said;
> we could leave that value as it is in Vanilla, but it makes no sense,
> IMO, if you program your game to deliver wands of charge=0 regularily.

Not all wands. Only /oW may have zero charges.

The rationalization behind that is (besides obvious game mechanics
changes) who would let a /oW lying around without using it?

> (But since I don't play UnNethack, as I've told, I really don't care
> much what your design decisions actually are. Just wanted to throw in
> my 0.02¤ in case you're interested in yet another thought.)

Thanks. I'm always interested in opinions from others. Even from
non-players. Helps to see how others view the changes made to the
code.

> [**] Which seems to me to be the essence; beginners won't reach that
> wand, regular ascenders don't really need it. How much does the game
> change for those players in between those two ranks? For them, is it
> really worth to guarantee just one or two wishes instead of three or
> four? Or reduce the number of wishes for the initial wand to plain 1
> instead of 1-3 (avg 2)?

Look at the problem from a different angle.

Beginners who get a wish don't know what to wish for.

Advanced players always wish for the same thing.

The latter is of course a part of the problem that a "standard
ascension kit" exists but nevertheless with the first /oW an advanced
player is set.

More wish variation is IMO a "Good Thing"[tm].

>> It think it could be sensible to up the probability of the
>> wrest-feature. That would mean less time spend wresting a /oW and more
>> of the other wands turning to dust accidentally. :-D
>>
>>> anyway as it's implemented in Vanilla; it's the most sensible, IMO.
>>
>> You mean 4 guaranteed useful wishes from any /oW?
>
> Make that three guaranteed wishes (if one doesn't go into the boring
> wrest-procedure, otherwise 4 wishes, yes, or more if its enchantment
> is higher).

Is holding a key for a few seconds really that tedious? Of course you
have to do it somewhere safe and if you're unlucky you burn 500 turns
but should be worth this.

>>> (Mind, you've advanced to and cleared the Castle to get that wand.
>>> Is it that bad to wish for some items to reduce boredom or get
>>
>> No, it isn't. That's the reason why I didn't axe wishes altogether.
>>
>> There are just way too many wishing sources in vanilla.
>
> Most are just unreliable faint chances (thrones, fountains), and the

For fountains I'd call it a small chance, not a faint.

There are some obsessive fountain quaffers out there and I've
witnessed many wishes early in the game from fountains.

> blessed smoky potions are only a good option if they are assigned to
> a specific and restricted set of potions in any specific game.

>>
>>> some helpful item that the stingy RNG refused to create randomly?
>>
>> That's what the castle wand or a random /oW is for.
>
> So do I see it. But in some games you need it (in contrast to helpful)
> even for necessary items, like MR.

You have to remember that UnNetHack also has the Blackmarket from
Slash'Em before the castle.

You usually find there a lot of useful stuff and a cloak of magic
resistance might get generated there.

>>> Refrain from wishing for the missing seven candles?)
>>
>> That's what magic lamp wishes are for.
>
> But, hey, don't fall into the kind of argument that everything is there
> if you just pick it up. That's simply not true. I had games without a
> single magic lamp and I had games with 4 (or even more?). You just can't
> rely on any particular item, whether it has a high probability or a low
> probability like a magic lamp.

One of NetHack's key features is its randomness. Of course the RNG
might work against you but as you said "wishless" isn't one of the
hardest conducts.

If for example you can't get MR from a cloak and from your quest item
you can still get dragon scales or polymorph into a dragon. Even
ascending MR-less is a possibility.

